Enzyme activity deppens on PH and temperature. PH has a strong inpact on enzyme activity passing through a maximum as PH increases. Similarly, enzyme activity also passses through a maximum as temperature increases. Enzymes show maximum activity at an \emph{optimum PH} and \emph{optimum temperature}. This maximum deppends on the nature of the enzyme and the catalyzed reaction. They reversibly loose activity at PH and temperature slighly lower or higher than their optimum. Enzyme deactivation is irreversible if the acidity or basicity is too high or if temperature is too high or low. One explanation for the PH effects is that ionization of protein carboxylic and amino groups occurs when PH is not taken too far within the optimum PH. Differently, enzymes loose their ternary structure hence loosing their activity at very low or high PH values or temperature higher than 30$^{\circ}$C. 

Because of their shape, graphs displaying catalyst activity vs PH or temperature are often referred to as vulcano plots.
Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-vulcano-plots} displays PH and temperature vulcano plots for the activity of amylase.

